  "headline": {
    "text": "Staff's view: the card against right-handers",
    "tone": "neutral",
    "basis": {
      "because": [
        {
          "label": "How the order is written",
          "value": "Ordering per The Book (Tango et al.): your three best hitters bat first, second and fourth, not third, fourth and fifth."
        },
        {
          "label": "What it is built from",
          "value": "Ranked on your scouts' view of each bat against right-handers: a projection from their grades, not this season's results."
        },
        {
          "label": "The bat",
          "value": "What your scouts' hitting grades against right-handed pitching say he'd hit, in points of wOBA above an average major-league hitter: +20 is a good regular, −20 a bench bat. It reads contact, power, eye and gap power the way they have predicted results in this league's history, and the order is built from it. Where a hitter has no split grades it is read on his overall grades, and says so. It isn't this season's production (read it beside OPS+ and wRC+), and it isn't OOTP's own valuation, which your front office can't see."
        },
        {
          "label": "The glove",
          "value": "Your scouts' 20–80 grade for him at the position he's assigned. Positions are chosen on the bat adjusted for the glove: the best bat plays a spot he can actually field, and a player only plays where the game has shown a grade for him."
        },
        {
          "label": "OPS+ and wRC+",
          "value": "OPS adjusted for league run environment and ballpark, scaled so 100 = league average. 130 means 30% better than average. Weighted Runs Created Plus — the most complete rate stat here. Total offense per plate appearance, park- and league-adjusted, where 100 = league average."
        }
      ],
